{"title":"Tessera Analytics","sub":"Growing 20% a year, but leaking customers","type":"Growth strategy","industry":"B2B software","geo":"United States","diff":2,"fmt":"Candidate-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"SaaS metrics · churn and net revenue retention · prioritisation","prompt":"Tessera sells analytics software to retailers on annual subscriptions. It has $30M of annual recurring revenue (ARR) and grew about 20% last year. The board wants 30% growth next year. The CEO wants to double the sales team. The COO thinks the problem is customers leaving. You have $3M of extra budget to allocate.","clarify":[{"q":"What does ARR mean here?","a":"The yearly value of all active subscriptions."},{"q":"New sales?","a":"About $9M of new ARR a year from new customers."},{"q":"Existing customers?","a":"Besides those who leave, existing customers downgrade about 3% of ARR a year and upgrade about 8%."},{"q":"Sales efficiency?","a":"Every extra $1 of sales and marketing brings about $0.80 of new ARR."}],"structure":{"root":"How can Tessera reach 30% ARR growth?","branches":[{"head":"Keep more existing ARR","items":["Churn by segment","Downgrades"]},{"head":"Grow existing customers","items":["Upgrades, more users"]},{"head":"Win new customers","items":["Sales capacity","Sales efficiency"]}]},"hypothesis":"ARR growth = new ARR − ARR lost + upgrades. If a big share of ARR leaves every year, adding salespeople just refills a leaking bucket. I'd find where churn is concentrated first.","questions":[{"q":"Here is churn by segment. Walk me through next year's ARR and the gap to target.","exhibit":{"kind":"table","headers":["Segment","Customers","ARR ($M)","Churn (% of ARR)","ARR lost ($M)"],"rows":[["Small business","1,200","6.0","40%","2.40"],["Mid-market","600","12.0","12%","1.44"],["Enterprise","200","12.0","6%","0.72"],["Total","2,000","30.0","15.2%","4.56"]],"title":"Exhibit 1 · Churn by segment, last year","note":null,"align":["l","r","r","r","r"],"boldLast":true},"answer":"<p>Lost to churn: $4.56M (15.2% of ARR). Next year: $30M − $4.56M − $0.9M downgrades + $2.4M upgrades + $9M new = <b>$35.9M</b>, 20% growth.</p> <p>Net revenue retention (what existing customers are worth a year later) is <b>90%</b>. Target: $39.0M, a gap of $3.1M.</p> <p><b>So-what:</b> small businesses are only 20% of ARR but <b>53% of the ARR lost</b>.</p>"},{"q":"Three ideas: (A) automated onboarding for small businesses, $0.5M, cutting their churn from 40% to 25%; (B) customer-success managers for mid-market, $1.0M, cutting churn from 12% to 7%; (C) more sales spend, $1.5M. Compare them.","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"396.533pt\" height=\"174.352pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 396.533 174.352\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\"><defs><style type=\"text/css\">*{stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: butt}</style></defs><g id=\"figure_1\"><g id=\"patch_1\"><path d=\"M 0 174.352 L 396.533 174.352 L 396.533 0 L 0 0 L 0 174.352 z\n\" style=\"fill: none\"/></g><g id=\"axes_1\"><g id=\"patch_2\"><path d=\"M 43.373 138.336 L 389.333 138.336 L 389.333 10.824 L 43.373 10.824 L 43.373 138.336 z\n\" style=\"fill: none\"/></g><g id=\"matplotlib.axis_1\"><g id=\"xtick_1\"><g id=\"line2d_1\"><defs><path id=\"mff7d576334\" d=\"M 0 0 L 0 3.5 \" style=\"stroke: #555555; stroke-width: 0.8\"/></defs><g><use xlink:href=\"#mff7d576334\" x=\"104.028325\" y=\"138.336\" style=\"fill: #555555; stroke: #555555; stroke-width: 0.8\"/></g></g><g id=\"text_1\"><g style=\"fill: #555555\" transform=\"translate(74.897153 154.03) scale(0.09 -0.09)\"><defs><path id=\"LibreFranklin-Regular-1\" d=\"M 352 0 Q 314 0 298 29 Q 282 58 294 109 L 1952 4659 Q 1971 4704 1996 4726 Q 2022 4749 2080 4749 L 2579 4749 Q 2694 4749 2726 4653 L 4365 109 Q 4378 70 4362 35 Q 4346 0 4301 0 L 3846 0 Q 3770 0 3744 64 L 3302 1306 Q 3290 1331 3277 1344 Q 3264 1357 3226 1357 L 1363 1357 Q 1306 1357 1286 1306 L 832 58 Q 826 32 803 16 Q 781 0 736 0 L 352 0 L 35